<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Kunigunde of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1740">1740</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Kunigunde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and elector of Saxony, and sister of Clemens Wenceslaus, the last elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. From 1776 she was princess-abbess of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> and Thorn, the last to hold either office. She lost Thorn when French forces overran it in 1794, and Essen when it was secularised and given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1803. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-kunigunde-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Maria Kunigunde of Saxony" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro Rotari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunigunde_by_Rotari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 10 November 1740, Maria Kunigunde was the sixteenth and youngest child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> and Maria Josepha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. She was taught Polish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, French, English, philosophy, geography, religion, drawing, music and dance, and sang the title role in Johann Adolph Hasse's opera Leucippo at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> court. In 1764 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> court proposed her as a bride for Archduke Joseph of Austria, later Emperor Joseph II. At a secret dinner at Teplice she hardly said a word, Joseph judged her too timid and married her cousin Maria Josepha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> instead, and the story made a suitable marriage almost impossible to arrange.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> demanded an imperial abbey for her as compensation, which would make her an Imperial Princess. Her admission at Münsterbilsen met fierce resistance from the chapter, but in 1775 she was elected coadjutor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> and Thorn, unanimously after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and Dresden paid 45,000 guilders to the voting canons and canonesses, and she succeeded Francisca Christina of Sulzbach on 16 July 1776. She spent most of her time at the court of her brother Wenceslas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, where he took almost no decision without consulting her, and governed her abbeys from a distance.</p><p>Her adviser Johann Jakob Schmitz pursued an enlightened absolutist programme that clashed with the chapter and estates; a forestry and hunting regulation of 1786 led to a suit before the Reichskammergericht. After Schmitz left in 1792, the compromise of 17 September 1794 gave the principality its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1931-constitution-of-ethiopia">first written constitution</a>. She also banned abortion, regulated surgeons and midwives, founded a school for upper-class girls and supported compulsory education. When the estates refused funds, she built a toll road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wesel">Wesel</a> and the Mark with a personal loan, later selling it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> for 45,000 thalers, and invested in ironworks such as Gute Hoffnung and St. Antony, a pioneer of heavy industry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the secularisation she kept her rank as a clerical sovereign and an annual stipend of 6,500 thalers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. She lived with Wenceslas, mostly at Oberdorf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, until his death in 1812, then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> to stay with her nephew Frederick Augustus. The last of her siblings to die, she was buried in the crypt of the Cathedral of the Holy Trinity. Her will of 1821, rediscovered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> state archives in 2001, left bequests to her household, from her Hofmeister to the cook.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maria Kunigunde of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Essen was secularised in 1803 and passed to Prussia; the last abbess was <b>Maria Kunigunde of Saxony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-essen">Imperial Abbey of Essen</a></li><li><blockquote>The last abbess was <b>Maria Kunigunde of Saxony</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-thorn">Imperial Abbey of Thorn</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.23,21.01&amp;year=1795" aria-label="Warsaw on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Maria Kunigunde of Saxony"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4531 5631h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4531 5631h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.23,21.01&amp;year=1795">What was here in 1795? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
